over, Virginia","confidence":0.88},{"name":"East Baltimore","confidence":0.82},{"name":"Johns Hopkins Hospital","confidence":0.78}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Henrietta Lacks’s cells transformed medicine, while their extraction and commercialization without her knowledge exposed deep racial and ethical injustices affecting her family.","confidence":0.94},"academicOrientation":{"score":32,"confidence":0.91},"profileConfidence":0.95,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"b2e5097dcf972c56abb7076562e1c774f535a2ac71d1b3075364af9b5872bbc8","reviewStatus":"flagged","validationWarnings":["central_places_hit_schema_cap"]},"primarySubject":"Medicine & Public Health","secondarySubjects":["Race & Ethnicity","Science"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily explores the intersection of medical ethics and public health through the lens of Henrietta Lacks' story, while also addressing themes of race and scientific discovery.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"9523fe9d8e6ba4c2fe9b35723a4f4e49b99913cdefccda11f7e1eaeb07468b8b","summary":"Her name was Henrietta Lacks, but scientists know her as HeLa. She was a poor black tobacco farmer whose cells—taken without her knowledge in 1951—became one of the most important tools in medicine, vital for developing the polio vaccine, cloning, gene mapping, in vitro fertilization, and more. Henrietta’s cells have been bought and sold by the billions, yet she remains virtually unknown, and her family can’t afford health insurance. This New York Times bestseller takes readers on an extraordinary journey, from the “colored” ward of Johns Hopkins Hospital in the 1950s to stark white laboratories with freezers filled with HeLa cells, from Henrietta’s small, dying hometown of Clover, Virginia, to East Baltimore today, where her children and grandchildren live and struggle with the legacy of her cells. The Immortal Life of Henrietta Lacks tells a riveting story of the collision between ethics, race, and medicine; of scientific discovery and faith healing; and of a daughter consumed with questions about the mother she never knew. It’s a story inextricably connected to the dark history of experimentation on African Americans, the birth of bioethics, and the legal battles over whether we control the stuff we’re made of.","pageCount":369,"publicationYear":2010,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Lacks, Henrietta, -- 1920-1951 -- Health","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-immortal-life-of-henrietta-lacks-rebecca-skloot"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Cancer -- Patients -- Virginia -- Biography","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-immortal-life-of-henrietta-lacks-rebecca-skloot"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"African American women -- History","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-immortal-life-of-henrietta-lacks-rebecca-skloot"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Human experimentation in medicine -- United States -- History","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-immortal-life-of-henrietta-lacks-rebecca-skloot"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"HeLa cells","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-immortal-life-of-henrietta-lacks-rebecca-skloot"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Cancer -- Research","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-immortal-life-of-henrietta-lacks-rebecca-skloot"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Cell culture","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-immortal-life-of-henrietta-lacks-rebecca-skloot"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Medical ethics","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-immortal-life-of-henrietta-lacks-rebecca-skloot"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Cancer","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-immortal-lif